<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russo-Swedish War (1590–1595)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1590">1590</a>–<a href="/years/1595">1595</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russo-Swedish War of 1590–1595 was fought between the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> under Feodor I, guided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Russia sought to recover the towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a> lost in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-teusina">Treaty of Teusina</a> of 1595 returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a>, Yam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koporye">Koporye</a> and Korela to Russia, while Sweden kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-swedish-war-1590-1595.jpg" alt="Russo-Swedish War (1590–1595)" width="200" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boris_Godunov_(Pushkinsky_zapovednik).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a> began the war in the hope of winning the lands of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, which had belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-plussa">Truce of Plussa</a> of 1583 had left Sweden in possession of Jama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koporye">Koporye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a> as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>. As soon as the truce expired early in 1590, a large Russian army led by Godunov and his sickly brother-in-law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodor-i-of-russia">Feodor I of Russia</a>, marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> towards Novgorod.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 18 January 1590 the Russians crossed the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>, defeated a Swedish detachment and laid siege to the castle of Narva, commanded by Arvid Stålarm. The fortress of Jama, or Jamburg, fell within two weeks, while Russian troops ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> as far as Reval (Tallinn) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> as far as Helsinki. On 25 February the Swedish governor Karl Henriksson (Horn) was forced to sign an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> surrendering Jama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koporye">Koporye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a>.</p><p>King John III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> rejected the settlement and sent a fleet against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a>, but the attack was checked by the Russian castellan. In summer 1591 the Swedes struck at Gdov and captured its governor, Prince Vladimir Dolgorukov. In the north, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-karelia">East Karelia</a>, Swedish raiders sacked Kola and other settlements near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a>. A party said to have been led by the Finnish peasant chief Pekka Vesainen destroyed the Pechenga Monastery on 25 December 1589, killing 50 monks and 65 lay brothers, and later burned Kandalaksha and a settlement at Kem, though it lacked the men to take the Kola Fortress or the Solovetsky Monastery.</p><p>Godunov's government sent Prince Volkonsky to pacify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, and the generals Bogdan Belsky, Fedor Mstislavsky and Prince Trubetskoy devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. After that the war settled into indecisive skirmishing until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> agreed to peace in May 1595.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-teusina">Treaty of Teusina</a>, also written Tyavzino or Täyssinä, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> renounced every claim on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s sovereignty over Estonia since 1561 was confirmed. Historians judge the outcome differently. Many scholarly works count it a Swedish victory or a result favourable to Sweden, and some call it a commercial victory for Sweden. Russian sources often claim a Russian victory and a peace on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s terms; the Cambridge History of Russia also speaks of a Russian victory while noting that not all Russian aims were met, and other writers see a military victory for Russia and a political draw.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russo-Swedish War (1590–1595)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
